[Music]  dream it up dream something bigger than  I did that's what my grandpa always told  me when I was small grandpa was born in  the late 1920s when Vietnam was still a  French colony life was tough starvation  was a constant fear growing up all my  grandpa could dream of was survival my  father was born just a year before the  friends finally left Vietnam for the  first time  Vietnamese believed the peas and  independence could be possible but then  the American came and for 21 years of  the Vietnam War more than 3 million  Vietnamese were killed like grandpa my  father dreamed of survival but this time  he dreamed of something else as well he  dreamed a peace and then me I was born  when neither the French nor the American  were out food was scarce but I grew up  in peace and like many others of my  generation I could afford to dream  something else something bigger I  dreamed of a good education three  generations three very different dreams  to many people it is a generational gap  to me it is a dream gap for most of my  life I've been obsessed with asking  people what their dreams are and having  asked hundreds if not thousands of  people the exact same question I know  for a fact that your background reims  in fact your background can dictate your  dreams  in other words the time you're born the  place you go up in the family you come  from can determine what your dreams are  can determine where your starting point  in life is which explains why more than  20 million people today are still at  risk of starvation still dream up their  next meal every single day which  explains why millions of people in  countries such as Syria and Yemen still  dream a peace which also explains why  million of kids still dream of going to  school so many years have passed but at  different places in different  circumstances millions of these people  still have the same dreams as three  generations of my family had years ago  the dream gap but my talk today is less  about why the dream gap but what we can  do about it I'm going to share with you  a story about of a 14 year old boy  Kelvin who lives in a slum outside of  Nairobi whom I met when I was leaving in  Kenya Kelvin so nuts now the street  where I left he got me really curious  because I saw him so nuts I'd take care  of his younger siblings who were always  by his side and sometimes big books too  I always bought nuts from Kelvin but one  day I asked him the question what's your  dream  Kelvin dream I have none after wish  though I wish I had enough money to buy  food for my five younger brothers and  sisters he went on to tell me about his  family but his father had left him a few  years earlier his mother was sick and at  the age of fourteen he was the  breadwinner of his family  it's people like Kelvin that have taught  me again and again to be curious about  the dreams of people around me as  different as we seem to be I felt  connected to him because in many ways he  has the same dream as my grandfather did  survival growing up I heard the saying  you are as big as your dream and I hated  it in fact I was afraid the people would  judge me for the dream I had but knowing  that your background has so much to do  with your dreams I would never judge  Kelvin or anyone for that matter for how  big or how small his dreams are its  people like Kelvin that we might me of  their dream can that we come from  different places we have different  dreams and that's okay one day I asked  Kelvin after you thought of going to  college he said that is something I  really wanted to do you probably call it  a dream but I'm not sure if I can make  it or afford it so I tell him about me  about my dream and how I pursued it and  I told him that I would help him pursue  his dream too so every Sunday at 4 p.m.  I would help Kelvin sell nuts on the  streets of Nairobi 100 packs 5 cents  apiece  5 dollars was what he needed to feed his  family and to afford to go to school and  in between I would teach him math it's  favorite subject it's times like this  with Kelvin that make me realize that  knowing somebody's dream is good but  helping them with their dream and  helping them build an extreme is even  better and helping is an S  early about giving money but it's about  giving them support giving them a little  push the day I left Nairobi for Stanford  it was a Sunday  I told Kelvin it's my last day selling  nuts with you I'm leaving tomorrow he  was surprised why are you leaving I told  him remember my dream good education I'm  stupid suing it I'm pursuing my and you  got to pursue yours too you see the  stories of people like Calvin on my  grandfather or father I saw the stories  of dreams dictated by their background  but look around there are so many Kelvin  still out there in the world still  facing the dream gap I hope that with  this story you will reach out to the  kelvins around you you were curious  about their dreams and their lives and  the stories and that you will give them  a little support on their journey to  achieve their dreams it's my grandfather  that told me to dream it up which has  let me hear a Stanford where a quote of  the G building which says dedicated to  the things that have not yet happen and  the people who are about to dream it up  if there is a place like this for me  there must be a place like this for the  kelvins allow all of us to thank you  [Applause]  you

